completely digital expansive unique  worlds spacecraft and creatures are now  pretty commonplace in blockbuster films  and even tv what's possible with visual  effects is incredibly impressive yeah  when i watch dune there's a very unique  quality to the way denis villeneux  builds and portrays his world on screen  what makes the visuals of dune stand  apart and feel different from almost  everything else i want to say up front  that while i'm a big fan of the grounded  realistic way that villeneuve puts his  visual effects on screen  i'm not saying that his approach is  inherently better than any of the others  i'm going to be comparing it to instead  what i want to try to showcase here is  how very small differences can add up to  change how we perceive the end product  on screen this video is sponsored by  mubi  look for example at this scene from  mcu's recent black widow this entire  sequence has pretty extensive green  screen work and one of the dead  giveaways is that the lighting on  natasha's face always remains fairly  flat and even and perfect regardless of  what's going on in the scene right here  natasha's face remains perfectly evenly  lit even while there's a huge explosion  going on behind her the uniformity of  this exposure actually kind of defies  the physics of what would be possible  with a camera and so it just naturally  has a sort of feeling of artifice to our  eyes compare the light on her face from  those moments with the light on her face  from these moments which appear to be  shot in an actual practical environment  see how the lighting on her face is  imperfect and actually has tiny shadows  that change  now check out this moment from dune the  explosion behind the characters throws  all of the characters into silhouette  this is what would happen naturally if  you actually tried to shoot this kind of  scene in real life the camera literally  wouldn't be able to properly expose both  the characters and the explosion behind  them while shooting the scene in dune  while they didn't have a practical  explosion behind the characters they  still created a real punch of light  behind them this ensured that the  lighting on the characters matched the  action of the scene both of these shots  use computer generated imagery my point  here is not that cgi or digital  compositing is bad the difference is how  the shots are lit and the one style of  lighting really grounds it within  reality and the other has a slightly  more comic cartoonish effect hawking and  sabotage slows us down one of the key  decisions in dune was to shoot  everything that takes place outside  actually outside in natural lighting or  sunlight this made shooting on certain  sets more difficult because they'd have  to construct a massive set and yet  somehow still protect it from the  elements and let in enough light to  shoot what they needed to this also  limited when they could shoot because  they'd have to wait for the sun to be in  the correct position but the intensity  and contrast provided by real sunlight  is incredibly difficult to produce in a  studio at such large scale a great  example of this is this scene  villeneuves and frasier's lighting in  dune is much more expressive and  contrasty than we're used to from these  kinds of films i'll touch more on  lighting later but there are a lot of  little subtle details like this that can  ground an artificial image in a sense of  reality  in dune sand and wind blowing everywhere  is another one that's frequently used in  these scenes even though everything  surrounding and behind the characters is  a digital set extension the sand and  wind blowing the characters is very real  the production actually used 18 tons of  sand and dust in the course of making  the film of course not all the sand  blowing in the wind in the whole film is  real a lot of it still had to be added  or enhanced in post but for vfx  supervisor paul lambert the holy grail  of visual effects is using them to  extend or enhance reality instead of  creating it completely from scratch so  even when they were using entirely  digital ornithopters they still went out  and shot footage of helicopters landing  and actually blowing sand around in the  wind sometimes they would just replace  the helicopter digitally with the  ornithopter and sometimes they would  just use that image of the sand blowing  in the wind for reference and recreate  it entirely somewhere else so it's not  that cgi is not capable of creating  realistic looking wind and sand it's  just that making it look super realistic  is a lot easier to do when you have  actual footage to reference another  great example of creating a small  practical effect and then expanding that  digitally is this sand displacement  effect that's used as worms are  approaching this was created practically  by placing a metal plate under the sand  that vibrated that effect can be then  expanded and enhanced digitally but it's  going to look realistic because the vfx  artists are going to be forced to match  what they're adding to the real effect  that was shot practically  often when shooting through windows into  vehicles like this crews will remove the  glass from the vehicle so that the shot  is cleaner and also so that they're not  dealing with reflections from the green  screen or blue screens then they add  back reflections and glass in  post-production but for dune these  scenes were shot with the windows  installed something they could actually  do because they didn't have to worry  about green or blue spill because they  were using the sand screens and then the  subtle but natural reflections that they  got from the sand screen in the windows  were enhanced using high resolution  footage of actual desert that they shot  with helicopters  one of the issues with surrounding your  characters or your set with a huge green  or blue screen is in a real environment  there's subtle lights that reflect and  bounce off of the objects that surround  the characters in the scene for example  right now my monitor right here is  reflecting a certain amount of light  onto my face and if i turn it off you'll  be able to notice the difference if i  hold a piece of paper near my face you  can easily see how much light is being  reflected onto my face by the paper so  when you surround a character by blue  screen or green screen you lose all of  those subtle natural reflections and  bounces that you would get off of a real  environment this is one of the distinct  advantages of the new wraparound led  system that's being developed and used  extensively on shows like the  mandalorian by surrounding a character  with a digital screen you actually get  light from that environment reflecting  and bouncing onto the characters greg  frazier the cinematographer for dune  also shot the first season of the  mandalorian and so he tested out those  led screens to see if it might be an  option for dune unfortunately led  screens aren't bright enough to create  the kind of high contrast sunlight blast  of light that would be in the kinds of  environments that they're creating for  dune so frazier villeneuve and vfx  supervisor paul lambert would have to  come up with a different solution if  they wanted to avoid using green screen  or blue screen the idea lambert  eventually came up with was to use sand  colored screens instead of blue or green  blue and green screens are usually used  because those are the easiest colors to  separate from the foreground but sand  colored screens were still close enough  and uniform enough that they could get  the job done and they had the added  benefit of casting a sand colored bounce  into the scenes for example when they  shot the scenes in the ornithopter they  surrounded them with these sand screens  instead of green screens and then the  light bouncing into the vehicles was the  appropriate color light reflecting from  the environment is an incredibly subtle  detail one that you won't really notice  unless you stop and really examine a  shot but it's these small details that  when you sacrifice them eliminate  elements of reality and lend a digital  composite a feeling of artifice  lighting isn't the only way visual  effects can defy physical reality when  you're shooting a scene that's  constructed entirely out of cgi are  mostly out of cgi you can create virtual  cameras that can move around in space  however you want you can see some good  examples of this in the snyder cut the  camera here is doing moves that would be  totally physically impossible or  extremely difficult if you were doing  them with an actual physical camera but  a digital camera can fly and twist  however you want and do it perfectly  smoothly again keep in mind i'm not  saying these kinds of shots aren't cool  or should never be used but by defying  physical possibility they remove a sense  of realism which is bad if realism is  your goal dune absolutely uses digital  cameras for certain shots so digital  cameras themselves aren't the issue here  but you're just not going to find these  kinds of physics-defying camera  movements in dune the creative team  intentionally avoided any kind of camera  movements that weren't grounded in  reality so in a scene like this you  could have the camera diving and  swooping with the ornithopter but the  camera stays fairly constrained and  moves in the broader more linear  movements that could actually be  accomplished with a helicopter if you  were filming this practically  in general as well the cinematography in  dune is just fairly constrained if dune  were made in the way that many modern  blockbuster films are it would be easy  to imagine a shot at this moment that  goes sweeping past the minor across the  dunes and towards the approaching worm  but villeneuve instead stays with the  characters i think this approach to  cinematography in general is also part  of what just helps everything feel more  grounded villeneuve is very careful with  his cinematography to pay close  attention to the relationship between  characters and objects in the world his  visual effects often include people for  scale or reference or are from the  perspective of characters in the scene  overall i think villeneuve's approach to  visual effects is actually quite  constrained you don't really get the  feeling that he's using the camera to  just show off the visual effects in many  moments you have really cool costume  design visual effects or ships and  they're just being almost completely  obscured by dust or smoke because that's  what would happen realistically if a  bunch of ships with whirly wings landed  in a completely sandy dusty environment  or in this moment you can't really make  out the benny jesuit ship because the  lights on the ship blow the image  completely into black in a sense you  have to sacrifice clarity of the visuals  and sometimes getting to see the coolest  element of the shot to create a shot  that looks like it would if you were  actually trying to shoot this thing in  reality  cgi has reached the stage where it can  create very realistic 3d objects but  creating visuals that feel grounded has  less to do with the creation of those  objects and more to do with the details  of the world that surround those objects  the lighting of the scene reflection on  windows blowing sand and dust and camera  movement are all details that frame and  contextualize the visual effects and  help them look real even with entirely  digital creations like the worm based on  interviews the aspect of the worm that  the vfx team was most nervous about  getting right was the movement of sand  around the worm  this grounded style isn't inherently  better than more stylized approaches to  visual effects but in an industry where  visual excess is a defining  characteristic of many blockbusters i  find this more minimalistic grounded  approach very refreshing and unique  and dune shows us that with care and 